{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgyfG6x5RJNzK6W8hCwCK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can tell you what the flag does, but the repo doesn't record why the release uses it.\n\n**What it does** (from `scripts/build.py`): it adds an extra file, `pkg/_legacy.py`, to the built bundle. That file makes `group_totals` and `parse_amount` also available under their old pre-1.0 names, `sum_by` and `to_decimal`. Without the flag, the bundle has the same code minus that file.\n\n**Why the release uses it:** nothing says. Unlike step 2, step 4 in the runbook has no \"Why:\" line. Git history doesn't help either: the flag only shows up in commit `548363d Release 1.3.2`, which looks like it brought in the existing runbook and build script, not the reason for them. Nothing else in the repo outside `build.py` mentions `sum_by`, `to_decimal` or `_legacy`, so nothing here needs it. That fits your \"cruft\" theory.\n\nBut the reason it would matter is outside this repo. The file exists so that code written against pre-1.0 names keeps working, so the question is whether anyone still imports `tally`'s `sum_by` or `to_decimal`. If someone does and you drop the flag, their imports break in the next release, and the tests here wouldn't catch it.\n\nSo I'd leave the runbook step alone until you or whoever owns the package confirms nobody uses the old names. If you decide to drop it, I can:\n- remove the flag from step 4, and\n- add a changelog entry under Unreleased saying the pre-1.0 aliases are gone, so anyone affected gets warned.\n\nAlternatively, if you want to keep it, I can add a \"Why:\" line to step 4 so this question doesn't come up again."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":224,"cache_read_input_tokens":20521,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":224},"output_tokens":3